  title="Is Your HVAC the Right Size? What Oversizing and Undersizing Cost You"
  description="Short cycles, sticky air, and a room that never cools are sizing symptoms. Here is how to tell what size system you have and why bigger is not better."
>
<p>If your air conditioner cools the house fast and then shuts off, and the air still feels clammy, the system is probably oversized. If it runs and runs on a July afternoon and never quite gets there, it is probably undersized, or the ductwork is. Both are common in Middle Tennessee, both shorten the life of expensive equipment, and both are usually created at the moment somebody replaced a system by reading the label on the old one instead of measuring the house.</p>

<p>Bigger is not better in HVAC. It is the single most expensive misconception in residential mechanical work.</p>

<h2>What size actually means</h2>

<p>Air conditioner capacity is measured in tons, where one ton is twelve thousand BTUs per hour of heat removal. Most single family homes here fall somewhere between two and five tons. Furnace capacity is measured in BTUs of output.</p>

<p>The right size is not a function of square footage alone, which is why the rules of thumb fail. It depends on insulation levels, window area and orientation, ceiling height, air leakage, duct location and condition, shading, and how many people and appliances are producing heat inside. Two identical floor plans, one with new windows and a sealed crawlspace and one without, do not need the same equipment.</p>

<p>The correct method is a load calculation, commonly called a Manual J, which accounts for all of that and produces a number. A proper job then sizes the ductwork to match, because a correctly sized unit attached to undersized ducts behaves like an undersized system.</p>

<h2>What goes wrong when the system is too big</h2>

<p>This is the more common error, because oversizing feels safe. It is not.</p>

<p><strong>Short cycling.</strong> An oversized unit hits the thermostat setpoint quickly and shuts off. Then the house warms slightly and it starts again. Starting is the hardest thing a compressor does, mechanically and electrically, so a system that starts six times an hour wears out faster than one that runs longer and less often.</p>

<p><strong>Humidity that never leaves.</strong> This is the one homeowners feel without knowing why. Air conditioning removes moisture by running long enough for warm air to keep passing over a cold coil and condensing. Short cycles do not give it that time. The result is a house that hits seventy two degrees and still feels sticky, so people set the thermostat lower, which costs more and fixes nothing. In our climate, where summer humidity is the actual comfort problem, this is a big deal.</p>

<p><strong>Uneven temperatures.</strong> Short runs do not move enough air long enough to reach the rooms at the end of the duct runs. The room over the garage stays hot no matter what the hallway thermostat says.</p>

<p><strong>Higher cost, twice.</strong> You paid more for the larger unit, and you pay more in wear and in electricity for the repeated startups.</p>

<h2>What goes wrong when the system is too small</h2>

<p>Less common, easier to notice, and not always the equipment's fault.</p>

<p>An undersized system runs continuously on design days and still loses ground in the late afternoon. It struggles most in the rooms furthest from the air handler. Continuous run time in extreme heat also means the compressor never rests, which is its own kind of wear.</p>

<p>The important nuance is that a system behaves undersized for reasons that have nothing to do with tonnage: undersized or leaking ductwork, a badly restricted return, a filthy filter, low refrigerant charge, or insulation that was never adequate. Replacing a correctly sized unit with a larger one because the house is hot is how a comfort complaint becomes a humidity complaint. Diagnose before you upsize.</p>

<h2>How to tell what you have, without buying anything</h2>

<p>Four checks, none of which require a technician.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Time the cycles on a hot afternoon.</strong> A properly sized system on a design day should run long, often most of the hour. Cycles under about ten minutes, repeating, point toward oversizing.</li>
<li><strong>Check the humidity.</strong> An inexpensive hygrometer tells you more than the thermostat does. If the temperature is at setpoint and indoor relative humidity is sitting well above the mid fifties in summer, the system is not dehumidifying, and short cycling is a leading suspect.</li>
<li><strong>Read the model number on the outdoor unit.</strong> Most manufacturers encode capacity in the model number, commonly as a number representing thousands of BTUs, where 24 is two tons, 36 is three, 48 is four, and 60 is five. Compare that against the house and against what a load calculation says.</li>
<li><strong>Walk the rooms.</strong> Consistent hot and cold rooms usually indicate a duct or airflow problem rather than a capacity problem, and that distinction changes the entire repair.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Two story houses, where one system is asked to do two jobs</h2>

<p>A large share of the newer housing around Nashville, Franklin, and Murfreesboro is two stories, and heat does what heat does. The upstairs runs warm in summer and the downstairs runs cool in winter, and a single system with a single thermostat cannot serve both well no matter what size it is.</p>

<p>Upsizing does not solve this. It makes it worse, because shorter cycles move even less air to the upstairs registers. The real fixes are airflow and control: balancing the dampers, correcting undersized returns upstairs, sealing duct leakage in an unconditioned attic where a startling amount of conditioned air is lost, and where the layout supports it, zoning the system or adding a second one. If a contractor's answer to a hot upstairs is a bigger condenser and nothing else, get a second opinion.</p>

<h2>The heating side is sized separately</h2>

<p>One more thing that gets skipped. The cooling load and the heating load are different calculations, and in a mild winter climate like ours they often point to different equipment sizes. Gas furnaces in particular are routinely installed far larger than the house needs, which produces the same short cycling story in reverse: fast blasts of hot air, uneven rooms, and more wear on the ignition and heat exchanger.</p>

<p>Heat pumps behave differently again, because the same equipment handles both seasons and is generally sized to the cooling load with supplemental heat for the coldest days. That tradeoff is worth understanding before you replace, since it changes both your equipment choice and your winter operating cost. Ask the contractor to show you both loads, not just the tonnage.</p>

<h2>Why so many systems are the wrong size</h2>

<p>Almost always the same story. The old unit failed, someone needed cooling back on that week, and the replacement was sized by matching the tonnage of the equipment being removed. If that unit was wrong in 1998, it is wrong now, and it just got locked in for another fifteen years.</p>

<p>It compounds when the house has changed. New windows, added insulation, a sealed crawlspace, or a finished bonus room all move the load, sometimes enough to change the correct size by a full ton. Replacing like for like ignores every improvement you paid for.</p>

<h2>Get it right at replacement, because that is the only cheap moment</h2>

<p>Sizing is fixed at installation. Once the equipment is in, your options are duct modifications, zoning, or living with it. So the day you replace a system is the day this gets decided for the next decade and a half.</p>

<p>Ask for four things before you sign anything:</p>

<ul>
<li>A load calculation for your house, not a tonnage matched to the old unit</li>
<li>An assessment of the existing ductwork, including whether the return is adequate</li>
<li>A written scope that names the equipment, the sizing basis, and what duct work is or is not included</li>
<li>A flat quote you can compare line to line against another bid</li>
</ul>

<p>What a complete quote should contain, and what a vague one is hiding, is covered in <a href="/articles/repair-or-replace-hvac-roof-water-heater/">repair or replace: HVAC, roof, and water heater</a>, which also walks the decision of whether you are replacing too early. If you are trying to figure out how much life the current system has left, <a href="/articles/how-long-do-home-systems-last/">how long home systems last</a> gives the realistic ranges for equipment in this climate.</p>

<h2>The cheapest thing you can do this year</h2>

<p>If the system is sized correctly and still underperforming, the fix is usually maintenance and airflow, not equipment. Filters, coil cleaning, refrigerant charge, and duct leakage account for an enormous share of the comfort complaints we see, and they cost a fraction of a replacement. The case for annual service, including what a real tune up covers, is in <a href="/articles/annual-hvac-service/">annual HVAC service</a>.</p>

